  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Tied through six innings until Ben Rice flipped it open late.",
    "what_this_means": "Rice's triple in the eighth broke a thin game when the Yankees needed exactly one swing. Will Warren gave them five innings and one run. Clean, efficient work that meant nothing if the bats didn't answer. They didn't have much to work with: eight hits, eight strikeouts, the kind of night that usually ends quietly. But the offense found its moment in the eighth, scored two, and held on through the ninth. Paul Blackburn closed it without drama. This is what stabilizing looks like. Not dominant, not flashy, just a team that knew when to hit and didn't fold when the margin was thin.",
